  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Consider the equation below\n$$f(x+f(y))=f(x)+f(y).$$\nIn this question we seek to determine which injective functions satisfy this equation for all $x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Show that $f(x)=x+c$ satisfies the equation.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x+f(y))=x+y+2c=f(x)+f(y)$.\n\nBoth sides simplify to the same expression.",
          "worked": "For $f(x)=x+c$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf(x+f(y))&=f(x+y+c)\\\\\nf(x+f(y))&=x+y+c+c\\\\\nf(x+f(y))&=x+y+2c.\n\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf(x)+f(y)&=(x+c)+(y+c)\\\\\nf(x)+f(y)&=x+y+2c.\n\\end{align*}$$\nBoth sides are equal, so $f(x)=x+c$ satisfies the equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Substitute in $x=0$, and separately $y=0$, to our original equation to show that if $f$ satisfies this equation, then $f(f(x))=f(x+f(0))$ for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(f(x))=f(0)+f(x)$ and $f(x+f(0))=f(x)+f(0)$.\n\nThus $f(f(x))=f(x+f(0))$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
          "worked": "Set $x=0$:\n$$f(0+f(y))=f(0)+f(y).$$\nRenaming $y$ as $x$ gives\n$$f(f(x))=f(0)+f(x).$$\nSeparately, set $y=0$ in the original equation:\n$$f(x+f(0))=f(x)+f(0).$$\nThe right-hand sides are equal. Therefore,\n$$f(f(x))=f(x+f(0))$$\nfor every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q15-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, conclude that any injective function which satisfies the given equation, must be of the form $f(x)=x+c$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=x+c$, where $c=f(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nInjectivity applied to part (b) gives $f(x)=x+f(0)$.",
          "worked": "By part (b),\n$$f(f(x))=f(x+f(0)).$$\nSince $f$ is injective, the inputs must be equal:\n$$f(x)=x+f(0).$$\nLet $c=f(0)\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Then $f(x)=x+c$. Part (a) shows that every function of this form satisfies the equation. It is also injective, since $a+c=b+c$ gives $a=b$. Hence these are exactly the injective solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
